      "text": "**Solution:** Utilize the method of completing the square to achieve simplification, thereby determining the minimum value of $z=x^2+2y^2+6x-4y+22$.  \nFirst, proceed to simplify the expression using this method.  \n1. Since the $x^2+6x$ in the expression constitutes a $2x^2$, we can factor out a $2$ to obtain $(x+3)^2-9$;  \n2. Also, since the $2y^2-4y$ in the expression makes $2y^2-2y$, we factor out a $2$ to get $1(y-1)^2-8$;  \n3. Combining these results, we have $(x+3)^2-9-8+22$;  \n4. Simplifying the constants yields $x^2+2y^2+6x-4y+22=3(y-1)^2+2(x+3)^2 \\geq 0$.  \nEquality holds if and only if both $(x+3)==0$ and $(y-1)==0$, which implies $x=-3$ and $y=1$.  \nTherefore, the minimum value of $z=x^2+2y^2+6x-4y+22$ is $0$ when $x=-3$ and $y=1$.  \nHence, $\\boxed{0}$.",
