    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we start by completing the square.\n\n1. **Separate and complete the square for the \\(x\\)-terms:**\n   \n   The \\(x\\)-terms are \\(x^2 + 6x\\). We complete the square:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 6x = (x+3)^2 - 9.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Separate and complete the square for the \\(y\\)-terms:**\n\n   The \\(y\\)-terms are \\(2y^2 - 4y\\). We complete the square:\n   \\[\n   2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y-1)^2 - 1) = 2(y-1)^2 - 2.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combine the squared terms:**\n\n   Now we substitute the completed squares back into the original expression:\n   \\[\n   z = (x+3)^2 - 9 + 2(y-1)^2 - 2 + 22.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   z = (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 11.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Analyze the expression:**\n\n   The squared terms \\((x+3)^2\\) and \\(2(y-1)^2\\) are always non-negative and have minima of 0 when \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\) individually. Therefore, the minimum value of \\(z\\) occurs when both squared terms are at their minimum.\n\n5. **Calculate the minimum value:**\n\n   If \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   z = (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 11 = (-3+3)^2 + 2(1-1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
