      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\n1. Start by completing the square for the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\(\\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2 = 9\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = \\left(x + 3\\right)^2 - 9. \\]\n\n2. Next, complete the square for the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nWe can factor out the 2 from the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y). \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\(\\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)^2 = 1\\):\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1. \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\n3. Substitute these completed squares back into the original expression:\n\\[ z = \\left(x + 3\\right)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the constants:\n\\[ z = \\left(x + 3\\right)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 9 - 2 + 22 = \\left(x + 3\\right)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe completed square form of the quadratic expression is:\n\\[ z = \\left(x + 3\\right)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nSince both \\((x + 3)^2\\) and \\((y - 1)^2\\) are squares of real numbers, both values are non-negative. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) will be achieved when both \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\((y - 1)^2 = 0\\).\n\nThus, setting \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) leads to \\( x = -3 \\), and setting \\((y - 1)^2 = 0\\) leads to \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = 0 + 2(0) + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
